A3.8 · Print how many White moves survived and add that number to the running White-moves-found total. Pass the included moves, with their reasons, flags, and popularity, to A4 to cover one by one.
 ^h2GwSqoV

Filter — run for each move in the pool

Work these out first:

1. Popularity = this move’s amateur games ÷ total amateur games here.
2. Master Threat flag? — at least 15 master games AND smoothed master White score ≥ 58%.
3. Floor bump — if the Master Threat flag is set and popularity is under 1%, set popularity to exactly 1%.
4. Amateur Trap flag? — at least 15 amateur games AND smoothed amateur White score ≥ 58% AND the Master Threat flag is not set.
5. The bar — 5% at moves 1–4, 10% at moves 5–8, 15% thereafter.

Then decide whether to include the move and set its reason, in this order — first match wins:

1. Popularity at or above the bar → include; reason: Mainline.
2. Otherwise, Master Threat flag set → include; reason: Master Threat.
3. Otherwise, Amateur Trap
flag set — or popularity ≥
1% and raw amateur White
win rate ≥ 55% → include;
reason: Amateur Trap, and
set the Amateur Trap flag
if it was not already set.
4. Otherwise → drop.

The flags were worked out before this decision and are not cleared. A move can therefore have reason Mainline while still carrying a Master Threat or Amateur Trap flag. A4 treats those flags as stronger than Mainline when tagging and counting.

Overriding all of the above: if the amateur pool is empty, nothing is included — not even a Master Threat.
 ^GykxSgzb

